The mechanism. Watch what a new machineʼs critics do, not only what
they write. Using the new tool while defending what the old one did well
isnʼt  hypocrisy;  itʼs  the  normal  sequence.  The  question  is  always  what
each method is actually for.
The rule. Choose a medium by the job it does. You donʼt get to sit out the
tools of your era on aesthetic grounds; you only get to choose whether
you use them to make something or merely to complain.
The practice.
Run the abbot test on yourself, once. For one week, tally two numbers
each day: hours spent consuming commentary about the transition,
and hours spent making anything at all. Donʼt moralize the numbers.
Just get them. Your ratio is your actual position, whatever your
opinions are.
Ship one made thing this week using a tool you have complaints about.
A document, a repaired fence, a video, a loaf, a raised bed. Size doesnʼt
matter. End the week as someone whose hands touched the new world
instead of someone who only reviewed it.
When you read the next elegant essay about AI ruining everything,
check the workflow before you absorb the thesis: how was it written,
published, and distributed? Then keep whatever part of the argument
survives. The abbot was right that some things deserved hands.
